# ⚠️ `_build` and `deps` are the load-bearing entries here, and their absence is
# not a slow build -- it is a broken one.
#
# `Dockerfile.isolation` compiles the dependency tree in its own cached layer
# and only then runs `COPY . .`. Without these two lines that copy lands the
# macOS-compiled artifacts on top of the Linux ones it just built, and a Mach-O
# object dlopen'd by a Linux runtime fails with "invalid ELF header". The image
# builds fine and the suite dies on load, which reads as a defect in the code
# under test rather than in how it got there.
_build/
deps/

.git/
.github/

# ex_doc output. 2.4 MB of generated HTML that is tarred into the daemon on
# every build, before any layer cache is consulted, and that nothing in the
# image reads.
doc/

# The built package tarball, for the same reason.
ex_sandbox-*.tar

*.log
.DS_Store
.elixir_ls/
.lexical/
cover/
node_modules/
.vscode/
.claude/

# CPython bytecode beside `priv/egress/nsacceptor.py`. The image runs the source
# under its own interpreter; a cache compiled by a different CPython is at best
# dead weight and at worst what gets loaded.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# NOTE: `docker/` is deliberately NOT ignored -- the Dockerfile COPYs the runner
# script, the census gate, the baseline and the systemd unit out of it.
#
# ⚠️ `docker/results` is: it is a bind mount at run time, so copying a previous
# run's census into the image is both pointless and a way for a stale artefact
# to be read as this run's.
docker/results/
